<!--
	GENERATED BY tools/make_mfg.py -- edit that, not this.

	Rapport's own facial expressions.

	The reason these exist: across the entire AAF install there are ten mfgSet
	references in total, and the pack that was playing when the owner first watched
	a Rapport scene has exactly one (Sleep_EyesClosed). The faces are stony because
	the animations never ask for anything else, not because anything is broken.

	Unlike the overlays, this layer costs nothing and depends on nobody: a morph id
	is an index into the engine's own facial morph table, so there is no texture to
	ship and no mod to credit. Rapport can drive faces on a completely vanilla
	install.

	The morph table, 0-49, is quoted from the comment block in
	CHAKPack_mfgSetData.xml -- the only place in the AAF install that writes it
	down. Every other pack ships bare numbers.

	lock="true" holds a morph against whatever else would move it. Rapport_Clear is
	the one set that does not lock, because its whole job is to let go.
-->

<!-- Everything back to zero and unlocked. Applied when a scene ends and when a save is loaded that was made during one, so nobody is left wearing a face Rapport put on them. -->
<mfgSet id="Rapport_Clear">
